Now let's evaluate each goal predicate:

1) candidate_clearance_established shift_candidate_beta: Not in init. Need to establish clearance for shift_candidate_beta.

2) candidate_compliance_certificate_linked shift_candidate_beta compliance_certificate_alpha: Currently shift_candidate_beta does not have compliance certificate linked. shift_candidate_alpha does. We need to assign compliance_certificate_alpha to shift_candidate_beta (maybe need to release from alpha first? Actually compliance_certificate_alpha currently linked to alpha; to link to beta we must release from alpha then assign to beta. But goal also requires candidate_compliance_certificate_linked shift_candidate_alpha compliance_certificate_alpha? Not required; but we may keep it? Not prohibited. However action assign_compliance_certificate requires compliance_certificate_available. Currently compliance_certificate_alpha is not available because it's linked to alpha. We'll need to release it first via release_compliance_certificate (action). Then assign to beta.

3) candidate_dispatch_slot_linked destination_availability_alpha dispatch_slot_alpha: Not in init. Currently only shift_candidate_alpha has dispatch_slot_linked. Need to allocate dispatch slot for destination_availability_alpha (and origin_availability_alpha and shift_candidate_beta). However note: action allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate requires dispatch_ready candidate and candidate_task_profile_linked and dispatch_slot_available, and not dispatch_allocated. For destination_availability_alpha we need it to be dispatch_ready, candidate_task_profile_linked (it is linked to route_task_profile_alpha), dispatch_slot_available. But dispatch_slot_alpha currently available? In init dispatch_slot_available dispatch_slot_alpha is true. However after we allocate to some candidate, it becomes not available. We need three allocations: destination_availability_alpha, origin_availability_alpha, shift_candidate_beta. But only one dispatch_slot_alpha object; we cannot allocate same slot to multiple candidates simultaneously because after allocation, slot becomes not available. However goal requires candidate_dispatch_slot_linked for each of those three with same slot. That seems impossible unless we can allocate then deallocate? But goal wants them linked at end. However maybe we can allocate slot to one, then later release? There's no action to release dispatch slot. The only action that affects dispatch_slot is allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate (makes slot not available) and execute_*_dispatch (makes slot available again). Indeed execute_*_dispatch effects: (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). So we can allocate, then execute dispatch (which dispatches the candidate) making slot available again, then allocate to another candidate, etc. At the end we need all three candidates to have dispatch_allocated true and candidate_dispatch_slot_linked true. But after execute_dispatch, the candidate becomes dispatched and dispatch_allocated? Let's see: execute_*_dispatch precondition includes (dispatch_allocated ?candidate) and effect adds (dispatched ?candidate) and (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit) and (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). It does NOT remove dispatch_allocated. So dispatch_allocated remains true. Good. So we can allocate slot to a candidate (makes dispatch_allocated true, slot not available). Then execute_dispatch for that candidate (requires dispatch_allocated true, etc.) which will make slot available again, but dispatch_allocated stays true. So we can allocate to another candidate later.

Thus we can achieve all three.

Goal also requires dispatch_allocated for each candidate: destination_availability_alpha, origin_availability_alpha, shift_candidate_beta. Already shift_candidate_alpha has dispatch_allocated true but not required. We need to allocate for those three.

Also dispatch_ready for each: need to make them dispatch_ready. For origin_availability_alpha and destination_availability_alpha we have actions mark_origin_availability_dispatch_ready and mark_destination_availability_dispatch_ready. For shift_candidate_beta we need to make it dispatch_ready via finalize_candidate_after_compliance_validation or finalize_candidate_after_sequence_validation or finalize_candidate_direct. Since shift_candidate_beta may not have route_sequence_context_set etc. We'll need to establish clearance etc.

Goal also requires dispatched for each: we can execute dispatch after making them dispatch_ready and allocated.

Goal requires origin_availability_confirmed origin_availability_alpha: currently false? In init we have origin_availability_node_linked but not origin_availability_confirmed. Actually init does not have origin_availability_confirmed. We need to confirm via unload_origin_payload? Wait origin_availability_confirmed is effect of unload_origin_payload (and also lock_origin_availability? Actually lock_origin_availability effect includes origin_availability_confirmed). Yes lock_origin_availability effect: (origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability) (origin_availability_confirmed ?origin_availability). So we can lock origin_availability_alpha to get both locked and confirmed. However goal also requires origin_availability_locked (already needed) and origin_availability_confirmed. So we need to lock origin_availability_alpha. However lock_origin_availability precondition: candidate_prepared ?origin_availability, candidate_operator_linked ?origin_availability ?operator_resource, origin_availability_node_linked ?origin_availability ?origin_node, origin_node_ready ?origin_node, not origin_availability_locked. We have candidate_prepared origin_availability_alpha? Not yet; goal requires candidate_prepared origin_availability_alpha (yes). Currently init: candidate_prepared origin_availability_alpha is false (only destination_availability_alpha and shift_candidate_alpha are prepared). So we need to prepare origin_availability_alpha via confirm_candidate_preparedness (requires candidate_created, vehicle_allocated, candidate_task_profile_linked, not candidate_prepared). We have candidate_created origin_availability_alpha true, vehicle_allocated origin_availability_alpha true (init), candidate_task_profile_linked? Not yet; we need to attach task profile to origin_availability_alpha. Then we can confirm preparedness.

Goal also requires origin_node_occupied origin_node_alpha and origin_node_ready origin_node_alpha. Init: origin_node_ready? Not present. origin_node_occupied? Not present. We need to load origin payload to make node occupied and ready? Actually loading origin payload makes origin_node_occupied true and origin_availability_locked true etc. Let's see: load_origin_payload effect: (origin_node_occupied ?origin_node) (origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability) (origin_payload_linked ?origin_availability ?payload_unit) (not cargo_available). It does NOT set origin_node_ready. To get origin_node_ready we need to activate_origin_node (effect: origin_node_ready). Precondition: candidate_prepared ?origin_availability, candidate_task_profile_linked ?origin_availability ?route_task_profile, origin_availability_node_linked ?origin_availability ?origin_node, not origin_node_ready, not origin_node_occupied. So we need origin_node not occupied and not ready before activation. But after loading payload, node becomes occupied, which would block activation. So perhaps we need to activate node first (make ready), then load payload (makes occupied and locked). However goal requires origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready both true. So we need both: after loading payload, node becomes occupied; we need also ready. Activation makes ready but requires not occupied. So we need to activate before loading, then after loading node becomes occupied but ready stays? Activation effect only sets origin_node_ready; does not clear it. So if we activate first (makes ready, not occupied), then load payload (makes occupied, retains ready? The load action does not affect origin_node_ready. So after load, origin_node_ready remains true (since not changed). So we can achieve both.

Thus sequence: activate_origin_node (requires candidate_prepared, candidate_task_profile_linked, origin_availability_node_linked, not origin_node_ready, not origin_node_occupied). Then load_origin_payload (requires candidate_prepared, origin_availability_node_linked, cargo_available, not origin_availability_locked). After load, we get origin_node_occupied, origin_availability_locked, and origin_payload_linked, cargo not available. Also origin_node_ready stays true.

Goal also requires origin_availability_confirmed (we get from lock_origin_availability? Actually load_origin_payload does not set confirmed. Lock_origin_availability sets both locked and confirmed. But after load_origin_payload we already have locked; we still need confirmed. We could instead use lock_origin_availability after loading? However lock_origin_availability requires origin_node_ready (true) and not origin_availability_locked (but after load it's locked, so precondition fails). So we need to get confirmed via some other action: unload_origin_payload sets origin_availability_confirmed true as well (effect). Actually unload_origin_payload effect: (origin_node_ready ?origin_node) (origin_availability_confirmed ?origin_availability) (cargo_available ?payload_unit) (not origin_payload_linked). So unloading also gives confirmed but clears payload link and cargo available. We need origin_payload_linked? Not in goal. So we could load then unload to get confirmed and cargo available again, but we lose payload link and occupied? Unload makes origin_node_ready (still true), origin_availability_confirmed true, cargo_available, and removes origin_payload_linked. It does not affect origin_node_occupied? Wait unload_origin_payload precondition includes origin_node_occupied, effect does not mention origin_node_occupied; so it remains occupied? Actually effect does not include origin_node_occupied, so it stays true (since not changed). So after unload, we have origin_node_occupied true, origin_node_ready true, origin_availability_confirmed true, cargo_available true, origin_payload_linked false. Good.

Thus we can load then unload to get confirmed while keeping occupied and ready. However we lose payload link (not required). Cargo becomes available again (goal requires cargo_available cargo_payload_alpha? Not in goal). So fine.

Alternatively we could use lock_origin_availability before loading? Let's see: lock_origin_availability sets locked and confirmed, but does not affect node occupancy. Precondition includes origin_node_ready (need ready). So we could activate node (make ready), then lock_origin_availability (requires candidate_prepared, candidate_operator_linked, origin_availability_node_linked, origin_node_ready, not origin_availability_locked). After lock we get locked and confirmed, but node still not occupied. Then we need to make origin_node_occupied via load_origin_payload (which requires not origin_availability_locked? Actually load_origin_payload precondition includes not origin_availability_locked. So after lock, origin_availability_locked true, so load_origin_payload fails. So we need to load before lock. So better to load then unload to get confirmed.

Thus plan: prepare origin_availability_alpha, activate origin node, load origin payload, unload origin payload (to get confirmed). That will give origin_availability_confirmed, origin_availability_locked (from load), origin_node_occupied, origin_node_ready, origin_payload_linked false, cargo_available true.

